 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "shropshire_carding_mill",
   "title": "Carding Mill Valley",
   "text": "Named after a mill that carded wool, combing the fibres straight for spinning, in the 1800s. The stream tumbles off the Long Mynd past a reservoir and a waterfall called the Lightspout. Victorians who came to take the air called Church Stretton 'Little Switzerland'."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "shropshire_long_mynd",
   "title": "The Long Mynd",
   "text": "A heather plateau about eleven kilometres long and over five hundred metres high, made of some of the oldest rocks in England. Gliders have soared off it since the 1930s, riding the wind that hits the hill and goes up. Wild hill ponies graze the tops."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "shropshire_devils_chair",
   "title": "The Devil's Chair",
   "text": "The biggest of the Stiperstones tors. The Devil, they say, sat here to rest while carrying an apron full of rocks; the strings broke and the stones spilled down the hill. When the mist covers the ridge, he is in his chair. If the Stiperstones ever sink into the ground, England will fall."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 44,
   "plaque": "shropshire_stiperstones",
   "title": "The Stiperstones",
   "text": "A ridge of quartzite, about 480 million years old, shattered into tors by the frost of the Ice Ages. Below it, the Snailbeach lead mines were worked from Roman times until 1955, one of the richest lead mines in Europe in its day."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 50,
   "plaque": "shropshire_wild_edric",
   "title": "Wild Edric",
   "text": "Edric was a real Saxon lord who held out against the Normans after 1066. Legend says he married a fairy wife, Godda, and still rides over these hills with her and his men before a war; lead miners swore they heard them knocking in the rock."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "WEST: the Stiperstones and the Devil's Chair. EAST: Carding Mill Valley and the Long Mynd. NORTH: Shrewsbury."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "shropshire_housman",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "A Shropshire Lad",
   "text": "A. E. Housman published A Shropshire Lad in 1896 at his own expense, after a publisher turned it down. Sixty-three short poems about lads, loss and the hills; soldiers carried it in their packs in the First World War. Housman grew up in Worcestershire and barely knew Shropshire; these hills were the blue edge of his boyhood sky, 'the land of lost content'. He borrowed an old rhyme for one poem: 'Clunton and Clunbury, Clungunford and Clun, are the quietest places under the sun.' His ashes lie at St Laurence's in Ludlow. The places are still quiet."
  }
 ],
